# ~/.skhdrc — skhd v0.3.9 + yabai v7.1.25
# Reload with: skhd --restart-service
#
# Full paths are used deliberately: skhd runs under launchd, which does not
# inherit your shell PATH, so a bare `yabai` may not resolve.
#
# LAYOUT OF THIS FILE
#   ctrl + <key>   -> spaces (always live, no mode)
#   tap caps_lock  -> enters "window mode", where bare letters act on windows
#   ctrl + cmd     -> send the focused window to a space AND follow it
#   ctrl + alt     -> send the focused window to a space and STAY
#   nothing on plain alt, because alt is reserved for app toggling
#
# ctrl+alt is shared with Karabiner's buttonless-drag chord, deliberately —
# see the note above those bindings for what that costs.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  MODES
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Syntax: :: <name> [@] [: <command run on entering>]
# The `@` makes the mode CAPTURE keypresses — anything not bound below is
# swallowed rather than passed to the app. That is what makes bare `h` safe
# to bind, and also why the on-screen indicator matters: without it you can
# be sitting in a mode that eats your typing.
#
# The indicator is a cheat sheet drawn centred on screen by Hammerspoon (hs.ipc
# is loaded in init.lua; yabaiHudShow/Hide are defined in mouse-drag.lua, which
# init.lua requires). It also parks the mouse out of the way while shown, since
# the mode is modal. If Hammerspoon isn't running these fail silently and the
# mode still works — you just lose the cheat sheet.
#
# THE CHEAT SHEET IS GENERATED FROM THIS FILE — nothing to keep in sync.
# init.lua re-parses it on every show, so edits appear immediately. Two
# markers drive it, both ordinary skhd comments so they change no behaviour:
#
#   #== <title>   starts a section on the sheet
#   #= <label>    the description shown for that binding
#
# A binding with no `#=` is simply left off the sheet. Rows whose command ends
# in `skhd -k escape` are detected automatically and marked as leaving the
# mode — that isn't annotated by hand either.
:: default : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudHide()' > /dev/null 2>&1
:: window @ : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudShow()' > /dev/null 2>&1
# Reached from window mode by pressing `m`. Same capture-all behaviour; the HUD
# switches to its move variant (title + follow/stay hint + space picker).
:: move @ : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1

# ── enter / leave ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRIMARY: a quick TAP on caps_lock. Karabiner's `to_if_alone` on that key
# emits f19 when caps_lock is pressed and released within 250ms with nothing
# in between; holding it is still plain Hyper, untouched and with no added
# latency (the `to` fires on key-down, `to_if_alone` only on key-up).
#
# f19 by keycode because skhd has no function-key literals — kVK_F19 = 0x50.
# No Mac keyboard has an F19 key, so nothing else can ever send this.
#
# `fn -` IS REQUIRED and is the whole reason a bare `0x50` silently does
# nothing. macOS tags every function-key event with kCGEventFlagMaskSecondaryFn,
# so the tap arrives as `code=0x50 flags=[fn]`, and skhd matches modifiers
# EXACTLY — a binding with no modifier can never match an event that has one.
# Verified with a Hammerspoon event log; Karabiner was doing its job all along.
#
# The other two are insurance for flag combinations we have not observed.
fn - 0x50    ; window
0x50         ; window
hyper - 0x50 ; window

# Four ways out, because being stuck in a capture mode is the failure case.
# Tapping caps_lock again toggles back out — same gesture in and out.
window < fn - 0x50    ; default
window < 0x50         ; default
window < hyper - 0x50 ; default
window < escape       ; default
window < q            ; default
window < return       ; default

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  WINDOW MODE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rule of thumb: navigation and layout tweaks KEEP you in the mode so you can
# chain them; property toggles EXIT afterwards via `skhd -k escape`, which
# trips the `window < escape` binding above.

#== navigate
# ── directional focus (stays in mode) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Move focus by DIRECTION rather than by app — the main payoff of tiling.
# DIR_SEL := north | east | south | west (doc/yabai.asciidoc:100).
# h/j/k/l sit in the same physical spots on AZERTY as on QWERTY.
window < h : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus west     #= focus
window < j : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus south    #= focus
window < k : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus north    #= focus
window < l : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus east     #= focus

# ── move windows around the tree (stays in mode) ──────────────────────────
# --swap exchanges two windows in place. (--warp is the other option: it
# re-parents the window, reshaping the tree rather than trading positions.)
window < shift - h : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap west   #= swap
window < shift - j : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap south  #= swap
window < shift - k : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap north  #= swap
window < shift - l : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap east   #= swap

# ── rotate the layout (stays in mode) ─────────────────────────────────────
# COUNTER-INTUITIVE: 270 is clockwise, 90 is counter-clockwise. The docs don't
# say which is which; this was derived from window_node_rotate() in src/view.c
# and confirmed by testing (a window on the right moves to the bottom under
# --rotate 270, which is a clockwise quarter turn). Don't "fix" these numbers.
#
# Only does anything on a bsp space — a float space has no tree to rotate.
window < r         : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --rotate 270   #= rotate clockwise
window < shift - r : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --rotate 90    #= rotate counter

# Reset all split ratios to equal, after dragging borders around.
# The shipped example (examples/skhdrc:89) uses `0`, but a literal 0 is
# unreachable on AZERTY without Shift, so this uses the physical key position.
window < 0x1D : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --balance   #= balance splits

#== window
# ── one-shot properties (invoke, then dismiss the HUD) ─────────────────────
# Fills the space with the focused window WITHOUT leaving your layout or
# triggering a macOS fullscreen animation. Press again to drop back in place.
window < f : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle zoom-fullscreen; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= fullscreen

# ── stateful toggles (flip and STAY, so the HUD chip updates in place) ─────
# These are on/off states you may want to flip and see reflected, so instead of
# leaving the mode they re-render the HUD (which re-queries state and inverts the
# row). Escape/caps still leaves when you're done.
#
# Sticky: the window follows you onto every space. Native yabai toggle.
# Flip the HUD chip FIRST (instant, from cache), then run the real toggle -- so
# the highlight tracks the keypress instead of waiting on yabai.
window < s : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudFlip("sticky")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle sticky   #= sticky

# Always on top: no native toggle in v7 (`topmost` was replaced by the
# `--sub-layer` setter), so this script reads the state and flips it.
# Beeps if there's no focused window. Chip flipped first, same as sticky.
window < t : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudFlip("topmost")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/toggle-topmost.sh   #= always on top

# Toggle tiling for the CURRENT space only (bsp <-> float). Other spaces keep
# whatever they were. Resets to the ~/.yabairc default when yabai restarts.
# The script sends the mode-leaving escape ITSELF, but only when restoring to
# float -- entering bsp keeps window mode (and the HUD) up so you can immediately
# tune the new layout with swap/rotate/balance.
window < b : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/toggle-layout.sh   #= bsp / float

# PIP: scales the window to fill the display, honouring your padding
# (window_manager.c:2410 -> scripting_addition_scale_window). Note it SCALES
# rather than resizes — the window's contents are magnified, they do not
# re-layout, which is what makes it different from zoom-fullscreen on `f`.
# Requires the scripting addition, so it silently does nothing if the SA has
# been lost after a Dock restart.
#
# On `i` rather than the `k` you asked for, because k is focus-north and part
# of the hjkl core. Move it if you prefer — it is this one line.
window < i : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle pip; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= pip (scale to display)

# ── nudge / size the focused window ───────────────────────────────────────
# The ARROW keys and SPACE are handled in Hammerspoon (mouse-drag.lua), not here.
# Arrows move the window through the Accessibility API for a smooth glide with no
# drop-shadow flicker (yabai's animated --move flickers the shadow); holding a key
# slides it, Shift dashes. Space cycles the window through centre / reasonable /
# almost-max / original sizes. The HS event tap swallows both while the HUD is up.

#== scratchpad
# ── scratchpad (exit after) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# A scratchpad is a labelled window you summon and dismiss from ANY space.
# Assigning one automatically makes it floating and unmanaged.
#
# Usage: focus the window you want (a terminal is the classic choice), press
# shift - p once to claim the "pad" label, then p from anywhere to show/hide
# it. Press shift - p again on that window to release the label.
window < p         : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle pad; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= show / hide
window < shift - p : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/toggle-scratchpad.sh pad; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= claim / release

# If a scratchpad ever gets lost off-screen, this forcefully brings every
# scratchpad window back to the front. Grouped with the other `p` bindings;
# ctrl is free to use here because the mode captures everything anyway.
window < ctrl - p : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --scratchpad recover; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= recover lost pads

#== spaces
# ── switch to a space by number, then leave the mode ──────────────────────
# Top-row AND numpad keys 1-9, bound by KEYCODE so they work on AZERTY without
# Shift (same reason 0x1D is used for `balance` above). No `#=` label on
# purpose: the HUD renders these as the space indicator row at the bottom
# (current bright, the rest dimmed), not as list rows. focus-space.sh beeps if
# the target space doesn't exist, matching the global ctrl bindings.
#
# The one labelled row here is `m`, which starts the move-to-space chord below.
window < m ; move   #= move to space (⇧ stay)
# tab cycles spaces WITHOUT leaving the mode, re-rendering so the indicator
# tracks the current space (wraparound like the global ctrl+arrow bindings).
window < tab         : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus next 2>/dev/null || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus first; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudShow()' > /dev/null 2>&1   #= next space (⇧ prev)
window < shift - tab : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus prev 2>/dev/null || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus last; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -c 'yabaiHudShow()' > /dev/null 2>&1
# top row -> kVK_ANSI_1..9 (5 and 6 are swapped; 7/8/9 jump around)
window < 0x12 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x13 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x14 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x15 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x17 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x16 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x1A : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x1C : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x19 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
# numpad -> kVK_ANSI_Keypad1..9 (0x5A skipped: that is F20, not a keypad key)
window < 0x53 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x54 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x55 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x56 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x57 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x58 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x59 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x5B : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x5C : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  MOVE MODE — reached with `m` from window mode
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Pick a destination space for the FOCUSED window:
#   digit         -> move & follow (land on that space with the window)
#   shift + digit -> move & stay   (window goes, you don't)
# Both top row and numpad, by keycode (AZERTY-safe). move-window-to-space.sh
# beeps if the target space doesn't exist or nothing movable is focused.

# ── ways out (cancel without moving) ──────────────────────────────────────
# `m` toggles move OFF: back to window mode (HUD stays, the m chip clears).
# escape / q / return / caps tap leave the HUD entirely.
move < m            ; window
move < escape       ; default
move < q            ; default
move < return       ; default
move < 0x50         ; default
move < fn - 0x50    ; default
move < hyper - 0x50 ; default

# ── move & FOLLOW: bare digit ─────────────────────────────────────────────
move < 0x12 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x13 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x14 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x15 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x17 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x16 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x1A : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x1C : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x19 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x53 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x54 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x55 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x56 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x57 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x58 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x59 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x5B : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < 0x5C : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"

# ── move & STAY: shift + digit ────────────────────────────────────────────
move < shift - 0x12 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x13 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x14 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x15 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x17 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x16 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x1A : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x1C : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x19 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x53 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x54 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x55 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x56 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x57 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x58 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x59 : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x5B : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
move < shift - 0x5C : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  SPACES — always live, outside any mode
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ctrl is safe to claim globally: these replace the macOS "Move left/right a
# space" shortcuts you disabled, and ctrl+digit isn't used by apps.

# ── switch spaces: ctrl + arrows ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Switching is instant (no Mission Control slide) because it goes through the
# scripting addition rather than the public API.
#
# The `||` fallback provides wraparound: `--focus next` errors with
# "could not locate the next space" on the last space, so we jump to the first.
# `2>/dev/null` only hides the harmless "could not locate the next space"
# message that yabai prints before the fallback runs — the wrap still happens.
ctrl - left   : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus prev 2>/dev/null || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus last
ctrl - right  : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus next 2>/dev/null || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus first

# ── jump straight to a space: ctrl + top row ──────────────────────────────
# Bound by HARDWARE KEYCODE, not by character. b∫On AZERTY the top row produces
# & é " ' ( § è ! ç à and the digits need Shift, so a literal `ctrl - 1` would
# be unreachable. Keycodes refer to the physical key position instead, so these
# work regardless of layout (and keep working if you switch to QWERTY).
#
# Keycodes are from kVK_ANSI_* in Carbon's Events.h. Note they are NOT
# sequential — 5 and 6 are swapped, and 7/8/9/0 jump around. Don't "fix" them.
#
# These go through focus-space.sh, which beeps (system alert sound) when the
# target space doesn't exist instead of failing silently.
ctrl - 0x12   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1   # & (kVK_ANSI_1)
ctrl - 0x13   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2   # é (kVK_ANSI_2)
ctrl - 0x14   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3   # " (kVK_ANSI_3)
ctrl - 0x15   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4   # ' (kVK_ANSI_4)
ctrl - 0x17   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5   # ( (kVK_ANSI_5)
ctrl - 0x16   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6   # § (kVK_ANSI_6)
ctrl - 0x1A   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7   # è (kVK_ANSI_7)
ctrl - 0x1C   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8   # ! (kVK_ANSI_8)
ctrl - 0x19   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9   # ç (kVK_ANSI_9)

# à = end of the physical row -> LAST space, mirroring Cmd+9 in browsers
# (which jumps to the last tab, not tab 9). Tracks your space count as it
# changes, and can never fail, so it never beeps.
ctrl - 0x1D   : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus last   # à (kVK_ANSI_0)

# ── the same, on the NUMPAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# kVK_ANSI_Keypad0..9. Note 0x5A is skipped — that is F20, not a keypad key.
#
# UNCERTAIN UNTIL TESTED: macOS tags keypad events with
# kCGEventFlagMaskNumericPad, and skhd matches modifiers EXACTLY — the same
# trap that made a bare `0x50` silently fail for f19. skhd has no `numpad`
# modifier to declare, so this works only if skhd masks that bit away rather
# than comparing it. Arrow keys carry an intrinsic flag too and `ctrl - left`
# works fine, which is the reason to expect masking. If these do nothing, the
# fallback is a Karabiner rule remapping the keypad to keys skhd can match.
ctrl - 0x53   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1   # keypad 1
ctrl - 0x54   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2   # keypad 2
ctrl - 0x55   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3   # keypad 3
ctrl - 0x56   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4   # keypad 4
ctrl - 0x57   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5   # keypad 5
ctrl - 0x58   : /Users/roelvangils/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6   # keypad 6
